@prefix prim:    <https://halcyonic.systems/atlas/primitive/> .
@prefix entry:   <https://halcyonic.systems/atlas/entry/> .
@prefix bearer:  <https://halcyonic.systems/atlas/bearer/> .
@prefix cco:     <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://halcyonic.systems/atlas/entries/bunge-1979-treatise>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://halcyonic.systems/atlas/atlas-core> ;
    rdfs:label "Atlas entries — Bunge 1979, Treatise on Basic Philosophy vol. 4"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Entries 002 and 003.

TWO entries from one source, deliberately. Bunge gives a general CHARACTERISATION
of system as an ordered triple, then explicitly says it is "not a definition
proper" and supplies a separate DEFINITION of concrete system. Collapsing them
into one entry would misrepresent him and would erase the caveat he himself
insisted on. The distinction is his, not ours."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Bearer
##############################################################################

bearer:bunge-1979-treatise-vol4
    a cco:ont00000253 ;
    rdfs:label "Bunge, M. — Treatise on Basic Philosophy, Vol. 4: Ontology II — A World of Systems (Reidel, 1979)"@en ;
    dcterms:creator "Mario Bunge" ;
    dcterms:date "1979"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:identifier "doi:10.1007/978-94-009-9392-1" .


##############################################################################
# Entry 002 — the ordered-triple characterisation
##############################################################################

entry:bunge-1979-ces-triple
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Bunge (1979), general characterisation: σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:bunge-1979-treatise-vol4 ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, §1.1 (the general concept of a system)" ;

    atlas:verbatim """One way of characterizing the general concept of a system is this. Let T be a nonempty set. Then the ordered triple σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩ is (or represents) a system over T iff C and E are mutually disjoint subsets of T (i.e. C ∩ E = ∅), and S is a nonempty set of relations on the union of C and E. The system is conceptual if T is a set of conceptual items, and concrete (or material) if T ⊆ Θ is a set of concrete entities, i.e. things.""" ;

    atlas:authorCaveat """However, the preceding is not a definition proper, because it does not tell us what exactly is the membership of the coordinates C, E and S of the ordered triple. We must therefore define the notions of composition, environment, and structure of a thing.""" ;

    atlas:includedExample "a theory: 𝒯 = ⟨P, B, ⊢⟩ — propositions P, embedded in a body of knowledge B, glued by entailment ⊢ (Bunge's own worked conceptual-system example)" ;

    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:composition ,
                           prim:environment ,
                           prim:structure ,
                           prim:set ,
                           prim:relation ,
                           prim:thing ;

    atlas:formalisedAs "Systems/Category/ShapeBunge.lean#BungeShape" ;

    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-01"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """⚑ "is (or represents)". Bunge's own parenthetical hedges between the triple
BEING a system and REPRESENTING one, and he resolves it later in the same chapter
by calling the triple "the minimal model of system σ at time t". This is direct
primary-text evidence on the is-versus-represents question — the distinction the
Lean encodings do not type. Klir's (T,R) IS the system; Bunge's ⟨C,E,S⟩ is a model
OF a thing. Same shape, different ontological status.

⚑ NOT the same triple as the informal gloss. The fuller form Bunge actually works
with is relativised to a class A of things and indexed by time: s_A(σ,t) =
⟨𝒞_A(σ,t), ℰ_A(σ,t), 𝒮_A(σ,t)⟩. Anyone citing "Bunge's CES triple" as a bare
3-tuple is citing the informal version.

⚑ CES, NOT CESM. Mechanism is not a coordinate here. The string "CESM" does not
occur in the 1979 text; mechanism becomes central in later Bunge (see How Does It
Work, 2004). Any citation pairing "1979" with "CESM" is an anachronism.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED: cco:ont00001808 (is about). Bunge is a realist and
would endorse the reading; asserting it anyway would still break the catalogue's
neutrality, which must not depend on which tradition an entry belongs to."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Entry 003 — Definition 1.1, concrete system
##############################################################################

entry:bunge-1979-def-1-1
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Bunge (1979), Definition 1.1: concrete system"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:bunge-1979-treatise-vol4 ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, Definition 1.1" ;

    atlas:verbatim """DEFINITION 1.1 An object is a concrete system iff it is composed of at least two different connected things.""" ;

    atlas:includedExample "a molecule, a coral reef, a family, a factory" ;
    atlas:excludedExample "a set of states of a thing" ;
    atlas:excludedExample "a collection of events, even if ordered" ;

    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:thing ,
                           prim:part ,
                           prim:bond ,
                           prim:action ;

    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-01"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """⚑ THE SEPARATING INSTANCE, IN BUNGE'S OWN WORDS. His excluded example is
"a collection of events, EVEN IF ORDERED". Klir, on the passage recorded in entry
001, holds that ordering a collection is exactly what makes it a system: "any
ordering of a set is a relation defined on the set". Two founders, both verbatim,
disagreeing about whether an ordered collection is a system. This is the highest-
value datum in the catalogue so far and it was found by reading, not by inference.

MACHINERY BEHIND THE REFUSAL (Definition 1.2, encoded here as primitives rather
than as its own entry, because it defines composition/environment/structure and
not "system"):
  𝒞_A(σ,t) = {x ∈ A | x ⊏ σ}
  ℰ_A(σ,t) = {x ∈ A | x ∉ 𝒞_A(σ,t) & ∃y (y ∈ 𝒞_A(σ,t) & (x ▷ y ∨ y ▷ x))}
  𝒮_A(σ,t) = relations, in particular bonds, on 𝒞_A(σ,t) ∪ ℰ_A(σ,t), with the
             bonding set 𝔅_A(σ,t) ≠ ∅
The nonempty-bonding requirement is what does the refusing, and it is encoded in
systems-science-foundations as ConcreteSystem.bondage_nonempty over the ActsOn
class (Bunge's ▷). The two encodings agree; that agreement should be recorded as
a mapping claim once the mapping layer exists, not asserted here.

RELATION TO ENTRY 002: Bunge presents 002 first and then disowns it as "not a
definition proper". 003 is the definition proper. They are not alternative
phrasings of one claim and must not be merged."""@en .
